  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 244 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nMild and dry conditions will slowly give way to cooler, more \nseasonable temperatures over the weekend into next week. Rain chances \nremain low through Thursday with chances increasing over parts of \nthe region as soon as Friday. More widespread potential arrives over \nthe weekend into next week. \n\nA more vigorous upper level shortwave and surface low moves west to \neast over the southern Canadian Provinces through the midweek period. \nThis system will send a cold front southeastward through the Upper \nMidwest Thursday. Despite the breakdown in the upper level ridge, \nsurface/mid-level ridging over the eastern U.S. maintains its \ninfluence with southerly flow encountering the slowing front near \nthe MO/IA border. The stalling front results in some spread with \nrespect to cloud cover, low rain chances (<20 percent), and \nconsequently high temperatures. What spread does exist is not \nterribly large (5 degrees) and within a narrow corridor across the \nfar northern sections of the CWA. Aside from KUIN, Thursday's NBM \ntemperatures have quietly inched within 5 degrees of record highs \n(86 at KSTL and KCOU). Forecast highs (low-80s) give some pause with \nrecent bias correction leaning a little too warm. Even at the 80 \ndegree threshold, HREF probabilities range 30-45 percent along/south \nof I-70. Records are unlikely in this setup, but warm temperatures \nand their persistence through late October remain notable.\n\nAs we progress from Friday into the weekend, rain chances gradually \nincrease, but model spread follow suit. Global guidance differs in \nthe a number of regional/small scale features, such as the eastern \nridge, speed/strength of a southern Plains surface low, and timing. \nHigher moisture initially pools along and south of the stalled \nboundary late Thursday into early Friday vorticity advecting out of \nthe southern Plains and hugging the northern side of the upper \nridge. Rain chances (30 percent) extend as far south as I-70 and \nincrease to near 70 percent over far northeast MO. Aerial extent of \nthe PoPs may be too broad as model sounding quickly dry further \nsouth with most location remaining dry and mild. \n\nA broad trough digs into the southwest CONUS over the weekend into \nearly next week, which ejects additional disturbances northeastward \ninto the central U.S. Rain chance continue to increase next weekend \ninto early next week. The best chances for widespread, soaking rain \nfavor late Sunday and early next week. Cooler, near normal \ntemperatures are favored to return.",
